Parallel computation of an inverted index
using the Map-Reduce paradigm

We used uthash.h to implement and manage an efficient hashmap in C,
which allows for fast storage and retrieval of words and file IDs
while processing data by Mappers and Reducers.

Main:

- Read the number of threads for Mappers, the number of threads for Reducers, and the file with the list of files to process

- Read the total number of files, and then each file in the list is
read and saved in a FileEntry array in the DataSync structure

- Allocate and initialize the shared data structure

- Create an array of pthread_t for threads and initialize an array of ThreadParams to send the necessary data to each thread

- Each thread is created and started with pthread_create (in a single thread of size mapper_counts + reducer_counts)

- The program waits for all threads to finish using pthread_join

(in a single thread of size mapper_counts + reducer_counts)

- After all threads have finished executing, the barrier, map_mutex, and task_mutex are destroyed

Map_reduce_function:

- Each thread receives a pointer to ThreadParams, which includes a pointer to
DataSync, the shared data structure. It allows each thread to access and
update the common data needed to handle map and reduce operations.

- Mapper threads:
-> dynamically take a file from the file list using task_mutex and curent_file_id
-> read the file content and extract the words
-> for each word:
-> formats it using format_word (remove non-alphabetic characters,
make it lowercase)
-> inserts it into its local hashmap along with the file ID
-> after processing the file copies the local content into word_hashmap,
using map_mutex for synchronization

- Reducer threads:
-> waits for all Mappers to finish (synchronization via pthread_barrier_wait)
-> each reducer processes a subset of the alphabet
-> for each letter:
-> selects words that start with that letter from word_hashmap
-> builds a local hashmap for the selected words
-> alphabetically sorts the words according to the number of files in which they appear
-> writes the results to a letter-specific file (a.txt, b.txt etc.)

Synchronization:

To ensure synchronization we used mutexes and barriers:
- map_mutex: is used to synchronize access to word_hashmap, which is
the global hashmap. Thus, only one thread (each Mapper) can
modify word_hashmap at the same time
- task_mutex: Is used to manage access to curent_file_id, which
represents the current index of the file to be processed. This mutex ensures
that only one thread can modify curent_file_id at the same time, thus allowing
dynamic distribution of files between Mappers
- pthread_barrier_t barrier: Is used to synchronize all threads,
both Mappers and Reducers. The barrier ensures that all Mapper threads have
finished processing the files and updated word_hashmap before the Reducer threads start processing the data
